# gesture.ges — a hand on one thing: press, drag, release, as a chart.
#
# The third chart `chart.ges` runs, and the first that is a *gesture*
# — `card:gui-is-difficult.md` §"Statecharts, executable and per
# subsystem", the `hand` drawn there, built 2026-09-08 for the roll's
# ruler (`Session._ruler_touched`, `Session.released`), which was the
# smallest of the four `if` ladders a hand on the roll ran as: one
# channel, one axis, one command at the end (`bars`).
#
# **The chart holds the time, the host holds the geometry.**  A
# gesture is state spread across time — press to release — and that is
# the only thing this file knows: what a touch means depends on whether
# one came before it, and a release means a click or a commit by the
# same rule.  Where the hand *is* — a tick, a key, a bar — arrives on
# the event as an `Int`, and what a `Preview` or a `Commit` does with
# two of them is the host's, because that is where the snap to a grid
# and the file live.  So one chart serves any hand on any one thing,
# and the ruler is its first host.
#
# **Its events are the wire's words.**  The window says `touched` for
# the press and for every move after it, and `released` once — it does
# not know which touch is the press, and the chart is where that is
# decided (`Idle` or not).  `Cancel` is the escape from anywhere; no
# wire sends it yet.

# ── The states, the touches, the acts ────────────────────────────────────

#: Nothing held; held where it took hold; carried from there to here.
#: Where it took hold rides along, so a drag is always measured from
#: the press and a release can say both ends.
Gesture := Idle | Pressed Int | Dragging Int Int

#: What the wire says: a touch at a place, the grab let go, or the
#: gesture abandoned.
Touch := Touched Int | Released | Cancel

#: What the host is asked to do.  `Take` on the press — show what was
#: taken and say so; `Preview` while carried — draw it where it would
#: land and say the interval; `Reveal` when let go without moving — a
#: click; `Commit` when let go after moving — the one command the
#: gesture was for; `Unpreview` — put the picture back.
Act := Take Int | Preview Int Int | Reveal Int | Commit Int Int | Unpreview

# ── The chart ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

#: Every hand starts empty.
gesture : Chart Gesture Touch Act
gesture = Chart Idle gestureStep gestureEnter

#: Nine arrows, all written — no catch-all, so a state and a touch with
#: no arrow between them is a missing arm the checker refuses.  A touch
#: while idle is the press; a touch while held is the drag, and the
#: place it carries is the latest; a release is a click from `Pressed`
#: and a commit from `Dragging`; a cancel lands idle from anywhere and
#: undoes the picture.
gestureStep : Gesture -> Touch -> Step Gesture Act
gestureStep Idle (Touched p) = Go (Pressed p) []
gestureStep Idle Released = Stay
gestureStep Idle Cancel = Stay
gestureStep (Pressed p) (Touched q) = Go (Dragging p q) [Preview p q]
gestureStep (Pressed p) Released = Go Idle [Reveal p]
gestureStep (Pressed p) Cancel = Go Idle [Unpreview]
gestureStep (Dragging p q) (Touched r) = Go (Dragging p r) [Preview p r]
gestureStep (Dragging p q) Released = Go Idle [Commit p q]
gestureStep (Dragging p q) Cancel = Go Idle [Unpreview]

#: Taking hold is an entry action of `Pressed`, so it happens once, on
#: the arrow in, and not on the press's own touch — which is how the
#: press and the drag stay one arrow each.
gestureEnter : Gesture -> List Act
gestureEnter (Pressed p) = [Take p]
gestureEnter g = []
